86045417] [17592186054873 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186055818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054873 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054873 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186054873 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a550baf0-8755-5225-8d5d-6b6a15c4056b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186052749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1069 "q-39821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e98c0c0b-7009-559f-859a-1e49b934344f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055252 #edb/ref 1074 "Squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3x} & =2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{2-3x})}^{2} & ={2}^{2} \\\\ 2-3x & =4 \\\\ 3x & =-2 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2-3(-\\frac{2}{3})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-\\cancel{3}(-\\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}})} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2-(-2)} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2+2} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}2 \\\\ 2 & =2\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-\\frac{2}{3}$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{2-3x}=2$  is $x=-\\frac{2}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055818 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055818 #edb/ref 1061 534127 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055818 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39bf5cfc-fba2-5011-ab69-295924eb9a74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055818 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055819 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055819 #edb/ref 1061 534128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055819 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2578baad-7192-5057-8d26-c29368c0cc0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055819 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055820 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055821 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055820 #edb/ref 1061 534131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055820 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1020b050-7a26-5d3e-b149-d1f8f6f2298b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055820 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055821 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055821 #edb/ref 1061 534132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055821 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89c128ce-cb37-56a9-8caa-d2032717e896" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055821 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054796 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055822 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055822 #edb/ref 1061 534135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055822 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f51fc950-6c70-5533-80f1-e63c82a706d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055822 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055823 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186055824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055823 #edb/ref 1061 534138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055823 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eb43bcad-ca2f-5d32-b84d-3157063c1495" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055823 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186055941 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055824 #edb/ref 1061 534139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055824 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6beca4aa-4e60-5c71-8b50-ff54c97cb605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055824 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186054798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055941 #edb/ref 1064 "When we want to solve a square root radical equation which has the square root on the left-hand side and a linear function on the right-hand side, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2x-1}=x,\n$$\n\nwe raise both sides to the power of $2.$ This allows us to get rid of the radical, and gives us a quadratic equation that is easier to solve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{2x-1})}^{2} & ={(x)}^{2} \\\\ 2x-1 & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that in square root equations, we should always check the obtained value(s) to ensure that they are actually solutions of the original equation.\n\nHere, we check that the obtained value $x=1$ is actually a solution of the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2(1)-1} & \\overset{?}{=}(1) \\\\ \\sqrt{1} & \\overset{?}{=1} \\\\ 1 & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{2x-1}=x$ has the solution $x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055941 #edb/ref 1065 14245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055941 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d17df195-3553-5a81-ac34-282046536fa2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055941 #edb/ref 1067 "Equations Containing Additional Linear Terms" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055984 #edb/ref 1064 "To solve a radical equation where the radical is isolated on one side of the equation, like\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{x+1} = 3,\n$$\n\nwe square both sides. Remember that squaring is the opposite of the square root, so $\\left(\\sqrt{x+1}\\right)^2 =x+1,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+1})}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x+1 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the equation in the standard way:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =9 \\\\ x & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check the result by plugging it into the original equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8)+1} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ \\sqrt{9} & \\overset{?}{=}3 \\\\ 3 & =3\\,\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=8$ satisfies the original equation, so we can accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the solution to $\\sqrt{x+1}=3$ is $x=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055984 #edb/ref 1065 3418 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055984 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "60edc96d-ba13-5542-b95d-c7e13ced8006" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055984 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055985 #edb/ref 1064 "Some radical equations have no solutions. For example, consider the radical equation\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3-x} = -2.\n$$\n\nThe square root of a number is always positive or zero. So in the equation above, the right-hand side is negative, while the left-hand side is always positive or zero. Consequently the two sides will never be equal, and there is no real solution.\n\nWe could also determine that there is no solution by using the same process as usual. If we try to solve this equation by squaring both sides of the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-x} & =-2 \\\\ {(\\sqrt{3-x})}^{2} & =(-2)^{2} \\\\ 3-x & =4 \\\\ x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, when we plug this result into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3-(-1)} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{4} & \\overset{?}{=}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\ne -2\\,\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe value $x=-1$ does *not* satisfy the original equation, so we *cannot* accept it as a solution.\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\sqrt{3-x}=-2$ has no real solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055985 #edb/ref 1065 3455 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055985 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c14f3a94-fdf5-5c7a-9929-101b5d933468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186055985 #edb/ref 1067 "Radical Equations with No Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186548098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186548121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186548135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving Radical Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186548098 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547296 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "5c07c81a-6299-5f56-a72a-da105bc2e7c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547348 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547348 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547348 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ef944fe-b546-5c3c-bb66-e324e8c66a3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547353 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-5,\\,-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547353 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547353 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "11994bd3-02cd-5d54-9e80-6bf4d1f9a271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547408 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-4,\\,3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547408 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547408 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2de55bb9-4e8d-5f06-b11e-556b8e8249ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547430 #edb/ref 1080 "x=3,\\,4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547430 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547430 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36d4014c-9f06-558b-b2b4-ea6a2f47a4db" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547501 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-5,\\,-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547501 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547501 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "518703a7-6d82-5deb-9823-04f8892b81fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547513 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-3,\\,4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547513 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547513 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "59872150-fbec-5d48-9fe6-1b9b9e554ed6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547552 #edb/ref 1080 "The equation has no solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547552 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547552 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6ad116cc-f346-5585-9c55-b508e61fb4e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547587 #edb/ref 1080 "x=-4,\\,-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547587 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186547